public lock (this)
{
    DimConfig ft = fieldTypes.Get(dimName);
    if (ft == null)
    {
        ft = new DimConfig();
        fieldTypes[dimName] = ft;
    }
    ft.RequireDimCount = v;
}
```
However, a better way to achieve thread safety in C# would be to use the `lock` statement or `ConcurrentDictionary`. 

Here is how you can do it with `ConcurrentDictionary`:

```csharp
private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, DimConfig> fieldTypes = new ConcurrentDictionary<string, DimConfig>();

public void SetRequireDimCount(string dimName, bool v)
{
    DimConfig ft = fieldTypes.GetOrAdd(dimName, _ => new DimConfig());
    ft.RequireDimCount = v;
}
```
In this code:

- `GetOrAdd` method checks if a key exists in the dictionary. If it does, it returns the corresponding value. If not, it adds the key with a new `DimConfig` as its value and returns the new value. 

Or using `lock` statement:

```csharp
private readonly object _lock = new object();
private readonly Dictionary<string, DimConfig> fieldTypes = new Dictionary<string, DimConfig>();

public void SetRequireDimCount(string dimName, bool v)
{
    lock (_lock)
    {
        DimConfig ft;
        if (!fieldTypes.TryGetValue(dimName, out ft))
        {
            ft = new DimConfig();
            fieldTypes[dimName] = ft;
        }
        ft.RequireDimCount = v;
    }
}
```

