```csharp
public void PollForUpdates() 
{
    Debug.Assert(Thread.CurrentThread.ManagedThreadId == Thread.CurrentThread.ManagedThreadId); 
    DoUpdates();
}
```

However, note that `Thread.CurrentThread.ManagedThreadId` will always return the same value for the same thread. It is likely you want to check if the current thread is the main thread. In that case you can use `SynchronizationContext` or another method.

A better solution could be:
```csharp
private SynchronizationContext _synchronizationContext;

public YourClass()
{
    _synchronizationContext = SynchronizationContext.Current;
}

public void PollForUpdates() 
{
    Debug.Assert(_synchronizationContext == SynchronizationContext.Current); 
    DoUpdates();
}
```
In this case, `_synchronizationContext` should be captured when your object is initialized on the main thread, and then you can compare it later.

Here is an example with more accurate naming:

```csharp
public class Updater
{
    private readonly SynchronizationContext _synchronizationContext;

    public Updater()
    {
        _synchronizationContext = SynchronizationContext.Current;
    }

    public void PollForUpdates() 
    {
        Debug.Assert(_synchronizationContext == SynchronizationContext.Current); 
        DoUpdates();
    }

    private void DoUpdates()
    {
        // your update logic
    }
}
```

